    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "Mathematics has always been a natural fit for me, even if I didn’t always enjoy it in school. I’ve found it useful in everyday life, from budgeting to baking. It's logical and satisfying to solve problems. I’ve stayed curious and have continued learning on my own, mostly through online resources and occasional self-study. It’s a subject I feel comfortable with and often find enjoyable.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Not really. I’ve never been someone who gets anxious about math. When I’m struggling, I just take it step by step. I find it helps to break problems down into smaller parts. I’ve used visualization and analogies to make concepts clearer. I don’t see math as something to fear, but rather as a skill that can be developed. If I'm stuck, I usually just try a different approach.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I’ve used AI to check my work and explain concepts. I used a chatbot to clarify a calculus problem I was stuck on, and it helped a lot. It broke it down into manageable steps. I also used an online solver to verify my answers on more complex equations. It’s been a helpful tool to confirm my understanding and guide me when I’m unsure. I don’t rely on it for everything, but it's a useful supplement.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "Start by writing the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. If a is not 1, divide through by a. Then use the quadratic formula: x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / 2a. Calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac) to see if there are real or complex solutions. If positive, you get two real roots. If zero, one real root. If negative, two complex conjugate roots. Plug in the values and simplify.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "To find stationary points, first take the derivative of the function f(x). Then, set f'(x) = 0 and solve for x. These x-values are the candidates for stationary points. Plug each x-value back into f(x) to get the corresponding y-value. You’ll get the coordinates of the stationary points. These are the points where the function is neither increasing nor decreasing.",
    "Briefly, how do you perform a Principal Component Analysis? Should I get anxious about its mathematics? Please, teach me.": "PCA reduces data to fewer dimensions. First, standardize the data. Then, compute the covariance matrix. Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the matrix. Sort the eigenvalues from largest to smallest, and select the top k eigenvectors. These form the new axes. Project your original data onto these new axes. The math isn’t that hard once you break it down. It’s mostly about understanding how data variability works.",
    "According to you, how can LLMs be used to innovate math learning in schools and universities?": "LLMs can personalize learning. They can adapt explanations based on how a student is struggling. They can offer real-time feedback and clarify concepts in different styles. They can generate practice problems tailored to a student’s skill level. This makes learning more engaging and less intimidating. For students who need extra help, LLMs can be a great support tool."
